[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:Too Early and Too Late]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.22]Lesson 60 | 第60课
[00:02.39]Too early and too late | 太早与太晚
[00:10.62]Why did the young girl miss the train? | 年轻的姑娘为什么没赶上火车？
[00:15.76]Punctuality is a necessary habit in all public affairs in civilized society. | 守时是文明社会一切公共事务中必不可少的习惯。
[00:23.31]Without it, nothing could ever be brought to a conclusion; everything would be in a state of chaos. | 没有它，任何事情都无法收尾；一切都会处于混乱状态。
[00:31.53]Only in a sparsely-populated rural community is it possible to disregard it. | 只有在人口稀少的农村，才可能无视它。
[00:38.44]In ordinary living, there can be some tolerance of unpunctuality. | 在日常生活中，对不守时是可以有一些容忍的。
[00:44.04]The intellectual, who is working on some abstruse problem, has everything coordinated and organized for the matter in hand. | 在研究某个深奥问题的知识分子，手头的一切都协调安排妥当。
[00:53.53]He is therefore forgiven if late for a dinner party. | 因此，他赴晚宴迟到是可以原谅的。
[00:58.07]But people are often reproached for unpunctuality when their only fault is cutting things fine. | 但人们常因不守时受到指责，而他们唯一的过错只是把时间抠得太紧。
[01:05.63]It is hard for energetic, quick-minded people to waste time, | 精力充沛、头脑敏捷的人很难容忍浪费时间，
[01:10.47]so they are often tempted to finish a job before setting out to keep an appointment. | 所以他们常常想在动身赴约之前把一件工作干完。
[01:16.25]If no accidents occur on the way, like punctured tyres, diversions of traffic, sudden descent of fog, they will be on time. | 如果路上没有发生事故——比如轮胎爆了、交通改道、突降大雾——他们会准时到达。
[01:25.88]They are often more industrious, useful citizens than those who are never late. | 他们往往比从不迟到的人更勤劳、更有用。
[01:32.04]The over-punctual can be as much a trial to others as the unpunctual. | 到得过早的人与迟到的人一样招人烦。
[01:38.12]The guest who arrives half an hour too soon is the greatest nuisance. | 提前半小时到达的客人是最令人头疼的。
[01:44.56]Some friends of my family had this irritating habit. | 我家的几个朋友就有这种令人恼火的习惯。
[01:48.32]The only thing to do was ask them to come half an hour later than the other guests. | 唯一的办法就是请他们比其他客人晚半小时到。
[01:54.09]Then they arrived just when we wanted them. | 这样，他们就会在我们正需要他们的时候到达。
[01:57.69]If you are catching a train, it is always better to be comfortably early than even a fraction of a minute too late. | 如果你要赶火车，舒舒服服地早到总比哪怕晚一分钟强。
[02:05.97]Although being early may mean wasting a little time, | 虽然早到可能意味着浪费一点时间，
[02:09.57]this will be less than if you miss the train and have to wait an hour or more for the next one; | 但这比误了火车、等上一小时甚至更长时间等下一班车损失要小；
[02:15.48]and you avoid the frustration of arriving at the very moment when the train is drawing out of the station and being unable to get on it. | 而且你还避免了火车正缓缓驶出车站时才赶到、却上不去车的沮丧。
[02:26.35]An even harder situation is to be on the platform in good time for a train and still to see it go off without you. | 更糟糕的情形是：你及时赶到了站台，却眼睁睁看着火车丢下你开走。
[02:36.02]Such an experience befell a certain young girl the first time she was travelling alone. | 一位年轻的姑娘第一次独自旅行时就经历了这种事。
[02:42.72]She entered the station twenty minutes before the train was due, | 她在火车到站前20分钟进了车站，
[02:47.20]since her parents had impressed upon her | 因为父母再三叮嘱她，
[02:49.96]that it would be unforgivable to miss it and cause the friends with whom she was going to stay to make two journeys to meet her. | 误了火车是不可原谅的，那会让即将收留她的朋友赶两趟路来接她。
[02:59.32]She gave her luggage to a porter and showed him her ticket. | 她把行李交给搬运工，给他看了车票。
[03:03.92]To her horror he said that she was two hours too soon. | 让她恐惧的是，搬运工说她早到了两个小时。
[03:09.16]She felt in her handbag for the piece of paper on which her father had written down all the details of the journey and gave it to the porter. | 她在手提包里摸索那张父亲写下了旅程全部细节的纸条，递给了搬运工。
[03:18.85]He agreed that a train did come into the station at the time on the paper and that it did stop, but only to take on mail, not passengers. | 搬运工承认确实有一班火车在纸条上的时间进站，也确实停靠，但只是为了装邮件，不载乘客。
[03:30.90]The girl asked to see a timetable, feeling sure that her father could not have made such a mistake. | 姑娘要求看时刻表，确信父亲不可能犯这样的错误。
[03:38.29]The porter went to fetch one and arrived back with the station master, | 搬运工去取时刻表，回来时把站长也带来了。
[03:43.19]who produced it with a flourish and pointed out a microscopic 'o' beside the time of the arrival of the train at his station; | 站长潇洒地掏出时刻表，指着火车到达他那一站的时间旁边一个小得几乎看不见的“o”字；
[03:53.04]this little 'o' indicated that the train only stopped for mail. | 这个小“o”表示那班火车只为邮件停靠。
[03:58.86]Just as that moment the train came into the station. | 正在那时，火车进站了。
[04:03.59]The girl, tears streaming down her face, begged to be allowed to slip into the guard's van. | 姑娘泪流满面，恳求让她溜进列车员的车厢。
[04:10.78]But the station master was adamant: rules could not be broken. | 但站长毫不妥协：规章制度不容破坏。
[04:17.08]And she had to watch that train disappear towards her destination while she was left behind. | 她只好眼睁睁看着火车朝着她的目的地驶去，而自己被留在了站台上。
